records.t:
Gump’s flight travels toward South Country (intended destination), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
“Then,” said Tip, addressing the Gump, “be kind enough to fly with us
to the Southward;

Tip is a boy, 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
“Then,” said Tip, addressing the Gump, “be kind enough to fly with us
to the Southward;

Jackdaws inhabit Jackdaw’s Nest (as a colony), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
for underneath them was a
monster nest, built by a colony of Jackdaws in a hollow ledge of rock;

Gump has existence duration first day (at the time of speaking), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
“Oh, as for that,” returned the Gump, “it is my first day of existence;
so I cannot Judge whether I am feeling well or ill.”

white silk tent is located before Emerald City (just before the city gates), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
Fortunately, the ground where the tent had been pitched, being Just
before the city gates, was hard and smooth;

Tin Woodman rules the Kingdom of the Winkies (as an absolute monarch), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
It isn’t a very big Kingdom,” said he to Ozma, “but for that very
reason it is easier to rule;

Mombi is captured captured, 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
When the wonderful tidings reached the ears of Queen Jinjur—how Mombi
the Witch had been captured; how she had confessed her crime to Glinda;

Saw-Horse has no neck, 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
Then he tried to look at himself; but he had, indeed, no
neck to turn;

journey to the Emerald City has destination Emerald City, 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
We are all going on a journey to the Emerald City, to see
His Majesty, the Scarecrow;

Emerald City is ruled by Scarecrow (as His Majesty), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
We are all going on a journey to the Emerald City, to see
His Majesty, the Scarecrow;

Saw-Horse has legs legs (with no knee-joints), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
His legs were
stiff and awkward, for there were no knee-joints in them;

Emerald City contains gems (beautiful and abundant), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
Moreover, the City glitters with beautiful gems, which might far
better be used for rings, bracelets and necklaces;

Guardian of the Gates of the Emerald City possesses the Guardian's keys (on a golden chain around his neck), 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
He carried a bunch of keys swung round
his neck by a golden chain;

the war between Glinda and Jinjur is secret secret, 1904-06, The Marvelous Land of Oz
You were not supposed to know it,” she returned, “for we have kept it
a secret;

Tin Woodman possesses axe, 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
“That is true,” agreed the Woodman. “My axe may be of service to her;
so I also will go with her to the Land of the South.”

Tin Woodman travels to Land of the South (with Dorothy), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
“That is true,” agreed the Woodman. “My axe may be of service to her;
so I also will go with her to the Land of the South.”

hope of returning to Kansas is lost Dorothy (after the balloon departed), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
Dorothy wept bitterly at the passing of her hope to get home to Kansas
again;

Dorothy travels with Tin Woodman (to the Land of the South), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
“That is true,” agreed the Woodman. “My axe may be of service to her;
so I also will go with her to the Land of the South.”

The Dainty China Country contains china barns, 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
There were also pretty little barns, with
china fences around them;

great spider has mouth a great mouth with sharp teeth (teeth a foot long), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
It had a great mouth, with a row of sharp teeth a
foot long;

Toto is a dog, 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
Dorothy could not see at all, but Toto
could, for some dogs see very well in the dark;

Toto can see in darkness (very well), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
Dorothy could not see at all, but Toto
could, for some dogs see very well in the dark;

forest is dark (under the trees), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
It was almost dark under the trees, for the
branches shut out the daylight;

Tin Woodman is saved by Tin Woodman's rescue (after standing immobilized in the forest), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
I might have stood there always if you had not come along,” he said;
“so you have certainly saved my life.

Tin Woodman is careful not to harm living creatures (as a habitual practice), 1900-04, The Wonderful Wizard of Oz
This made the Tin Woodman very unhappy, for he was always
careful not to hurt any living creature;

the Sawhorse has legs four wooden legs (shod with gold), 1908, Dorothy and the Wizard in Oz
The legs
of the Sawhorse were four sticks driving into holes bored in the log;

the Tin Woodman repairs the Sawhorse (making a new leg and ear), 1908, Dorothy and the Wizard in Oz
Then the Tin Woodman cut a straight and strong limb from a tree with
his gleaming axe and made a new leg and a new ear for the Sawhorse;

the Sawhorse is named Prince of Horses (by Princess Ozma after winning the race), 1908, Dorothy and the Wizard in Oz
My friend, I reward you for your swiftness by proclaiming you Prince
of Horses, whether of wood or of flesh;

Dorothy has Eureka (as her kitten in this book), 1908, Dorothy and the Wizard in Oz
In this book Dorothy had to take her kitten with her
instead of her dog;

wax mannequin has complexion wax, 1905, The Woggle-Bug Book
Her complexion was lovely, for it was
wax;

Mr. H. M. Woggle-Bug, T. E. has size about the size of a man (in his magnified state), 1905, The Woggle-Bug Book
Being about as big, in his magnified state, as a man, the Woggle-Bug
took care to clothe himself like a man;

Paris fashion influences Wagnerian Plaids (the gown is a last year's Paris model), 1905, The Woggle-Bug Book
Indeed, it was the latest (last year's) Paris
model, although the Woggle-Bug did not know that;

readers' letters request more about Dorothy, 1907, Ozma of Oz
Their sweet
little letters plead to know "more about Dorothy";

Nome King is a king (of the Nomes), 1907, Ozma of Oz
"Come along, then, my dear," said the King, climbing down from his
throne with some difficulty, because he was so fat;

Billina is praised by the red rooster (for her cluck and cackle), 1907, Ozma of Oz
The red rooster has often said that my cluck and my cackle were quite
perfect;

breakfast is unavailable Dorothy (while adrift), 1907, Ozma of Oz
"I'm beginning to get hungry," remarked Dorothy.  "It's breakfast time;
but there's no breakfast."

Tiktok speaks in a monotonous clock-work voice (with syllables separated), 1907, Ozma of Oz
The words sounded a little hoarse and creaky, and they were uttered all
in the same tone, without any change of expression whatever;

the Land of Oz has no record of starvation, 1910, The Emerald City of Oz
No one ever starved to death in Oz," declared Dorothy, positively;
"but people may get pretty hungry sometimes."

Bunnybury's rabbit inhabitants dislikes dogs (above all things on earth), 1910, The Emerald City of Oz
"In the first place they would frighten our people, who dislike dogs
above all things on earth;

Uncle Henry is old old, 1910, The Emerald City of Oz
Oh, he is old, and has worked enough in his lifetime," interrupted the
girl Ruler;

Dorothy belongs to the Land of Oz (permanently, after being brought from Kansas), 1910, The Emerald City of Oz
You're in the Fairyland of Oz, now;
an' what's more, you belong to it!

the Land of Oz is a Fairyland of Oz, 1910, The Emerald City of Oz
You're in the Fairyland of Oz, now;
an' what's more, you belong to it!

Cap'n Bill is a prisoner in the Peony Room (as the old man-fish), 1911, The Sea Fairies
"Take the mermaids and the child to the Rose Chamber," commanded Zog;
"and take the old man-fish to the Peony Room."

Zog's slaves number hundreds, 1911, The Sea Fairies
All the slaves of Zog, hundreds in
number, were assembled in the room;

Zog's slaves originates in the earth (all were born there), 1911, The Sea Fairies
At one time or another," he said, "all of us were born upon the
earth and lived in the thin air;

Merla escorts Cap'n Bill (during entry into the palace), 1911, The Sea Fairies
while Merla followed just behind them,
escorting Cap'n Bill.

King Anko has Anko's extreme age and length (a source of pride), 1911, The Sea Fairies
I feel I have much to be proud of," continued Anko, in a dreamy tone;
"my great age, my undisputed sway, and my exceptional length."

the fleet of argonauts is a shell-dwelling sea creature (able to hide in its shell), 1911, The Sea Fairies
The argonauts live in shells, in which
they are able to hide in case of danger from prowling wolf fishes;

Scoodlers has two fronts two fronts (with no back), 1909, The Road to Oz
It has a face both front and back," whispered Dorothy, wonderingly;
"only there's no back at all, but two fronts."

Scoodler heads are removable (the Scoodlers throw them), 1909, The Road to Oz
The angry Scoodler at once picked off his head and hurled it at the
Rainbow's Daughter;

Johnny Dooit has grey hair, 1909, The Road to Oz
His hair was
grey, his whiskers were grey;

Dyna possesses the Magic Powder (in a small bottle, without knowing it was magical), 1909, The Road to Oz
Among them was a small bottle of the Powder of Life;
but of course Dyna didn't know it was a Magic Powder, at all.

Toto is friendly with Cowardly Lion (he knows and loves him), 1909, The Road to Oz
Toto barked at the Cowardly Lion in joyous greeting,
for he knew the beast of old and loved him,

Dorothy is a Princess (of the Emerald City), 1909, The Road to Oz
Dorothy willingly agreed, being the only other Princess in the Emerald
City;

Allegro da Capo has toot (always destitute of flats or other errors), 1909, The Road to Oz
My toot is always destitute
      Of flats or other errors;

music has charms soothing savage (according to Allegro da Capo), 1909, The Road to Oz
Music hath charms, and it may
  Soothe even the savage, they say;

Land of Oz has ruler Our Ruler, 1913-07-01, The Patchwork Girl of Oz
Our Ruler's a bewitching girl whom fairies love to please;
    She's always kept her magic sceptre to enforce decrees

Glass Cat has pink brains (visible and able to work), 1913-07-01, The Patchwork Girl of Oz
Also I have some lovely pink brains;
you can see 'em work.

Ozma loves Jack Pumpkinhead, 1913-07-01, The Patchwork Girl of Oz
Ozma loved the stupid fellow, who had
been her earliest companion;

the Hoppers have houses and streets marble (handsome exterior appearance), 1913-07-01, The Patchwork Girl of Oz
because you judged from appearances and they have handsome marble
houses and marble streets;

the Blue Forest is a forest (solitary and forgotten), 1913-07-01, The Patchwork Girl of Oz
they would always have lived in the solitary Blue
Forest;

the owl's poems describe Scraps (as lacking sense and loving fun), 1913-07-01, The Patchwork Girl of Oz
"Patchwork Girl has come to life;
    No one's sweetheart, no one's wife;
    Lacking sense and loving fun,
    She'll be snubbed by everyone."

Scraps is a Patchwork Girl (living and unconventional), 1913-07-01, The Patchwork Girl of Oz
"Patchwork Girl has come to life;
    No one's sweetheart, no one's wife;

palace of Queen Cor contains red-domed chamber (a chamber with green dragons painted on its walls), 1916, Rinkitink in Oz
"She is in the chamber with the red dome, where green dragons are
painted upon the walls," replied Inga;

the magic boat has exterior color lustrous black, 1916, Rinkitink in Oz
On the outside it was painted a lustrous black,
without any other color to relieve it;

Betsy Bobbin is friend of Hank (they became good friends aboard the ship), 1914, Tik-Tok of Oz
On board the ship, during the days that preceded the wreck, when the
sea was calm, Betsy and Hank had become good friends;

ship has part deck, 1914, Tik-Tok of Oz
They had to crowd close together, for their support was only a
hatch-cover torn from the ship's deck;

raft (Betsy's and Hank's raft) is a hatch-cover (torn from the ship's deck), 1914, Tik-Tok of Oz
They had to crowd close together, for their support was only a
hatch-cover torn from the ship's deck;

Cap'n Bill loves Trot (since she was a baby), 1915, The Scarecrow of Oz
He loved the baby and often held her on his lap; her first
ride was on Cap'n Bill's shoulders, for she had no baby-carriage;

Ku-Klip made Tin Woodman's tin body (from replacement tin parts), 1918, The Tin Woodman of Oz
Nothing remained but for me to make them new parts out of tin;
but, as you see, tin answered the purpose very well

Ku-Klip made Tin Soldier (from replacement tin parts), 1918, The Tin Woodman of Oz
Nothing remained but for me to make them new parts out of tin;
but, as you see, tin answered the purpose very well

Soup Sea is ruled by Soup King, 1922, Kabumpo in Oz
“Ho! I am the King of the Soup Sea,
    Yes, I am the King of the Deep;

rescue of Ozma and Dorothy is required immediate action (the sooner the better), 1920, Glinda of Oz
Of course we must rescue them," she continued, "and the sooner they
are rescued the better pleased they will be;

Crunch's transformation into a statue is a statue (standing forever in Mudge), 1923, The Cowardly Lion of Oz
for a punishment this Stone Man shall stand forever in Mudge,
a monument to your greediness and folly.

the Mudgers have bright blue whiskers, 1923, The Cowardly Lion of Oz
if it were not for their bright blue whiskers,
you would take them for Arabs

Urtha wears flowery skirts, 1924, Grampa in Oz
First fell Urtha, her flowery skirts fluttering
gracefully around her;

Tattypoo rules Gillikens (Good Witch of the North), 1928, The Giant Horse of Oz
For instance, the Northern Country of the Gillikens is governed by the
Good Witch Tattypoo;

Emerald City's golden gate is part of Emerald City, 1927, The Gnome King of Oz
“To the Emerald City’s golden gate;
    Home! Home at last, I can hardly wait!”

Skyrocket have the top, 1930, The Yellow Knight of Oz
making a dash for the Skyrocket jumped in;
pulling the Princess after him, he slammed down the top

Jack Pumpkinhead possesses Forbidden Flagon (carrying it in one hand), 1929, Jack Pumpkinhead of Oz
It was Jack Pumpkinhead, clasping the precious
flagon in one hand and holding to his head with the other;
